# Telework & International Remote Work Policy – v2 (February 2026)

Approved by the Management Committee on 26 January 2026, after information of the works council.

## 1. Structural telework (Belgium)

1.1 Employees whose job allows it may telework structurally up to **3 days per week** from their home address.
1.2 Structural telework requires a written telework annex to the employment contract, in accordance with **CCT n° 85** (place of telework, days, equipment, costs, availability, return to the office).

## 2. Occasional telework

Occasional telework (e.g. personal circumstances, strikes) is possible after agreement with the manager.

## 3. International remote work (NEW)

3.1 Employees may work remotely from another EU/EEA country or Switzerland for a maximum of **60 working days per calendar year** (all stays added up).

3.2 Each stay requires the **prior written approval of the HR Director**, requested at least **6 weeks in advance** with form HR-INT-01. Approval by a manager or HR Business Partner alone is not sufficient.

3.3 Before approval, HR checks:
- **social security**: an A1 certificate must be obtained before departure when the employee will work in the other country (Reg. (EC) 883/2004); without A1 the stay cannot start;
- **tax**: stays that could create tax liability abroad (183-day rule, permanent establishment) are refused;
- **labour law and data security**: VPN mandatory, no client data stored locally.

3.4 Stays beyond 60 working days may exceptionally be granted by the HR Director with an amended telework annex.

3.5 International remote work outside the EU/EEA/Switzerland is not allowed.

## 4. Cross-border workers

Employees residing in a neighbouring country (e.g. Luxembourg, France, Netherlands) must respect the telework thresholds applicable to their situation (social security framework agreement on cross-border telework and bilateral tax agreements). HR monitors the number of telework days.

## 5. Costs

See the Home Office Allowance Policy.

Catherine Lemaire, HR Director

